1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,000
In this video, we're going to take a look at setting the initial test scope in Burp Suite.

2
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:17,000
By default, Burp will log all HTTP or WebSocket messages for further analysis.

3
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:22,000
Setting a scope allows you to tell Burp which URLs you do and don't want to test.

4
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:26,000
For example, you might want to exclude URLs you don't have permission to test,

5
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:31,000
or URLs that aren't safe to test as they might contain dangerous functionality,

6
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:38,000
or simply that they're not relevant and you want to reduce the noise and concentrate on what's important.

7
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:52,000
To add a URL to your scope, go to Scope Settings via the Target tab or the Main Settings.

8
00:00:52,000 --> 00:01:03,000
In the Target Scope section, under Include in Scope, click on Add and specify the prefix for the URLs you want to match.

9
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:09,000
When you choose OK, you'll be asked whether you want to stop logging out of scope traffic.

10
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:12,000
You can alter this option later if you change your mind.

11
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:18,000
For now, we're going to disable it as this can provide performance benefits.

12
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:26,000
After you've added a URL to your scope, you may want to exclude specific paths that are a subset of the In Scope prefixes.

13
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:31,000
For example, you may want to exclude an admin page from your tests.

14
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:37,000
To do this, in the Target Scope setting, under Exclude from Scope, click on Add.

15
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:47,000
And now you're ready to start testing.

16
